{
 "id": "2017-negrin-campus",
 "name": "Campus Places: Short Perceived Restorativeness Scale",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: each participant is randomly given one of nine photographs of places on the University of La Laguna campus (Tenerife), which expert judges had classified as low, medium or high in restorativeness (three each). With the photo visible throughout, they answer the five-item short Perceived Restorativeness Scale (being away, fascination, coherence, compatibility, scope; 0 = not at all to 10 = totally), plus pleasure and preference. Photos are from Figures 1–3 and the short-scale items as printed in the Methods of Negrín, Hernández-Fernaud, Hess & Hernández (2017), Frontiers in Psychology 8: 1735 (CC BY 4.0); the paper's Table 1 means are stored for comparison, and its raw data are on Harvard Dataverse. Differences from the original study: (1) the 26-item Spanish PRS that participants also answered is not included, because its items are not printed; (2) the pleasure and preference questions are described but not printed in the paper, so their wording is ours; (3) online and in English instead of a projected photo and paper questionnaire in Spanish in a class session; (4) photo assignment is random per participant rather than one class group per photo; (5) the student question is added.",
 "author": "Negrín, Hernández-Fernaud, Hess & Hernández",
 "year": "2017",
